def are_collinear(p1, p2, p3):
    def slope(p, q):
        # Returns (dx, dy) for points p and q
        return q[0] - p[0], q[1] - p[1]
    # Three points are collinear if the slope of (p1, p2) equals that of (p1, p3)
    return slope(p1, p2) == slope(p1, p3)
```

If p, q and r lie on a straight line, then the vector from p to q is parallel to the vector from p to r, and the two vectors are scalar multiples of each other, so they have equal direction ratios and equal slope. Comparing the whole pairs instead of just one component is safer than comparing the result of dy ÷ dx, which fails at vertical lines and at divisions; it also avoids floating-point approximations. The third sample, with all three points the same, gives (0, 0) on both sides, so the equality holds and the function returns True.

