def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    px, py = p
    ax, ay = a
    bx, by = b

    if ay == by:
        # Horizontal segment; p must lie on the same horizontal line and between the x-coordinates
        return py == ay and min(ax, bx) <= px <= max(ax, bx)
    elif ax == bx:
        # Vertical segment; p must lie on the same vertical line and between the y-coordinates
        return px == ax and min(ay, by) <= py <= max(ay, by)
    else:
        # General case: compute the parametric value and check the range
        dx, dy = bx - ax, by - ay
        t = (px - ax) / dx
        if t < 0 or t > 1:
            return False
        return abs((ay + t * dy) - py) < 1e-12
```

The general case handles the most common situation. If the points are vertical or horizontal, it checks the axis the segment runs along and uses a direct bound comparison, otherwise it normalises the line segment to the unit interval and confirms that the y-coordinate agrees with the parameterisation up to a small numerical tolerance (`1e-12`). Every branch allows the endpoints themselves by covering the boundaries of the comparisons. In Python 2 floating-point division is guaranteed accurate on integers, so no cast is needed beyond a decimal point, and the spacing is safe for any segment not parallel to an axis. The special-case logic would suffice on its own, but the parametric form makes for a complete and reasonably compact solution.

