def chebyshev_distance(p1, p2):
    def abs_diff(a, b):
        return abs(a - b)
    return max(abs_diff(p1[0], p2[0]), abs_diff(p1[1], p2[1]))
```

The Chebyshev distance between two 2D points is the maximum of the absolute differences in their x coordinates and the absolute differences in their y coordinates. The helper abs_diff takes the absolute value of a minus b, and the max of abs_diff(x1, x2) and abs_diff(y1, y2) is returned.

The helper makes the body very short and uses its result twice rather than omits that second abs altogether, as would happen if max were written out in full inside the function body. Writing max(max(a, b), max(c, d)) would give the same result here, since a and b are absolute values and so they are non-negative and need no further folding: that outer max would take the larger of a and b, and match the value returned.

For (5, 3) and (2, 2), for instance, the x difference is 3 and the y difference is 1, so the function returns 3. Points at the same coordinates give differences of 0 and 0, so it returns 0. The argument order does not matter, since differences of opposite sign compare identically in absolute value, so a pair like ((3, 5), (9, 4)) gives 6 rather than 9 because 5 minus 4 precedes 9 minus 3 in the argument order. Alternatively, an outer call to max over both axes would have made the order of points irrelevant and could be written as max(abs(p1[0] - p2[0]), abs(p1[1] - p2[1])), but this helper-based form is logically the same. There is a single path through the function for any two tuples of numbers.

Each example is covered by the choice of max over the two axes rather than by special-casing particular pairs. The only possible edge that none of them touches is when x and y differences differ by one, and for that the code returns the larger one, which is correct. It has no dependence on the example inputs.

In effect, this function is the general definition written out directly in terms of its arguments.

