The specification asks for every horizontally or vertically touching pair of cells to hold different values, with exactly two distinct values arranged so that cells at positions whose row index plus column index is even hold one value and those with an odd sum hold the other. A grid with no cells, or a single cell, counts as valid.

The function scans the grid row by row and compares each cell to the value its checkerboard parity "needs". Starting from grid[0][0], every cell that sits on an even row + column must hold the value that is one minus the most recent 'needs' collected, and every cell on an odd sum must hold 1 minus the most recent 'needs' collected. The formula uses (row + col) % 2 as the parity and 1 - expected as the colour alternating against each cell. The expected value starts at grid[0][0] and is updated after every cell. An inner loop walks over the cells until no new cells are visited in a pass, which means either the grid is empty or every cell has already been seen and the pattern holds.

In the one-cell case, the parity is 0, so the cell holds the value determined by its expected starting value and the scan exits early, because no new cell is visited. In the early [[2], [2]] example for a 2x1 grid, the first row holds 2 and the parity is 0, the second row sits on parity 1 and expects 1 - 2 = -1, so the scan returns False, which is correct since the two vertical neighbours share the same value. The three example grids match the function's decisions, but any valid two-colouring of the plane gets the right answer.

The walkthrough version could be one-liner by collecting all even-parity cells and odd-parity cells from every position and confirming that each set holds exactly one value and the two sets differ. This version is slower but simpler: it never writes to the grid and only records the parity of a position against the colour that cell holds, so it does not impose any order. For a real application, the parity-parity flat walk would be more efficient.

